Ah, I see exactly why that happened!
This is actually a beautiful example of how smart your React frontend is. I added row.getTxnType() to check if it was a Credit, but **your React frontend already converts Credits into negative numbers** before it even sends the JSON payload!
Because React already does the math (-55000.00 for Credit, 55000.00 for Debit), your BatchPostingDto.JournalRequestRow doesn't even need a txnType variable.
We can completely remove that check and just add the amount directly to the balance.
### The Fix
Open your VoucherCommandService.java and look inside that createBatchRequest method.
**Find these two lines (around line 230):**
```java
            // 2. Add to Group Balance (Debits are positive, Credits are negative)
            BigDecimal signedAmount = "Credit".equalsIgnoreCase(row.getTxnType()) ? amount.negate() : amount;
            balancingMap.put(compositeKey, balancingMap.getOrDefault(compositeKey, BigDecimal.ZERO).add(signedAmount));

```
**Replace them with just this one line:**
```java
            // 2. Add to Group Balance (React already sends Credits as negative numbers, so we just add them!)
            balancingMap.put(compositeKey, balancingMap.getOrDefault(compositeKey, BigDecimal.ZERO).add(amount));

```
Make that quick swap and the compilation error will vanish immediately!
